1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:10,162
"The nightly evaluation scored 0.800 last
Wednesday and 0.400 today, on the same

2
00:00:10,202 --> 00:00:14,723
model checkpoint. Is it the model,
the data or our own harness?"

3
00:00:15,063 --> 00:00:20,133
You start where everyone starts: what changed
between the last good release and now.

4
00:00:20,173 --> 00:00:24,756
The diff, the list of lines removed and
added between two snapshots, runs to

5
00:00:24,796 --> 00:00:28,832
hundreds of lines. One commit alone
touches 40 lines in five files.

6
00:00:28,872 --> 00:00:33,880
It's a formatter run. Somewhere in or around
it there may be one line that matters.

7
00:00:34,220 --> 00:00:38,985
If you read that diff top to bottom, you'll
be tired before you reach it, and you

8
00:00:39,025 --> 00:00:43,611
may not recognise it when you do. There
is a way to read a large diff from the

9
00:00:43,651 --> 00:00:47,763
outside in, and to make Git remove
the noise before your eyes have to.

10
00:00:47,803 --> 00:00:50,551
Keep those 40 lines in
mind. We'll shrink them.

11
00:00:52,391 --> 00:00:56,622
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:56,662 --> 00:01:00,333
This video opens the module
on history investigation.

13
00:01:00,373 --> 00:01:05,795
The textbook's central fact for the whole
module: history stores snapshots and

14
00:01:05,835 --> 00:01:11,467
parent links, nothing else. A commit is
one saved snapshot of the project, and it

15
00:01:11,507 --> 00:01:13,777
names the commit it was built on.

16
00:01:14,117 --> 00:01:18,685
Diffs, renames, "who wrote this line" and
"which commit broke it" are not recorded

17
00:01:18,725 --> 00:01:23,294
anywhere. Git computes them when you ask,
with heuristics that have thresholds and

18
00:01:23,334 --> 00:01:27,902
blind spots. An investigator has to know
which answers are facts read from objects

19
00:01:27,942 --> 00:01:29,307
and which are inferences.

20
00:01:29,647 --> 00:01:33,330
Today that principle is applied
to the most basic tool, the diff.

21
00:01:33,370 --> 00:01:37,740
Four things. Summaries and filters, which
answer "what changed" before "how".

22
00:01:37,780 --> 00:01:41,005
Renames and copies, which are
computed and can be missed.

23
00:01:41,045 --> 00:01:44,727
Whitespace and word-level views,
which read through reformatting.

24
00:01:44,767 --> 00:01:49,023
And diff algorithms, which decide how
one and the same change is displayed.

25
00:01:49,063 --> 00:01:53,204
At the end comes --check, a small guard
that belongs before every commit.

26
00:01:53,544 --> 00:01:58,573
Every command today is 🟢 SAFE. Nothing
is written. The project is scorekit.

27
00:02:00,413 --> 00:02:01,992
Here's what you'll walk away with.

28
00:02:02,332 --> 00:02:03,744
After this video you can:

29
00:02:04,084 --> 00:02:09,369
Summarize a diff by files, by status and by
counts, and filter it by kind of change.

30
00:02:09,709 --> 00:02:13,004
Explain a rename score and
change the detection threshold.

31
00:02:13,344 --> 00:02:16,808
Separate real changes from
whitespace and reformatting noise.

32
00:02:17,148 --> 00:02:19,766
Compare two diff algorithms
on the same change.

33
00:02:20,106 --> 00:02:24,333
Find whitespace errors and leftover
conflict markers before committing.

34
00:02:26,173 --> 00:02:31,170
Summaries and filters. A patch between two
releases can run to thousands of lines.

35
00:02:31,210 --> 00:02:36,023
Start with a summary and descend from
there. All of these options work with git

36
00:02:36,063 --> 00:02:40,753
diff, git show and git log alike, because
the three share one diff machinery.

37
00:02:41,093 --> 00:02:45,129
--stat draws one line per
file with a scaled histogram.

38
00:02:45,169 --> 00:02:51,057
--shortstat keeps only the total. --numstat
prints the same numbers unscaled and

39
00:02:51,097 --> 00:02:55,578
tab-separated, the form for
scripts. --name-only lists paths.

40
00:02:55,618 --> 00:02:58,468
--name-status adds one letter per path.

41
00:02:58,808 --> 00:03:04,067
--diff-filter selects by those letters.
An uppercase letter includes, a lowercase

42
00:03:04,107 --> 00:03:05,113
letter excludes.

43
00:03:05,453 --> 00:03:10,685
Renames and copies. In one sentence: a
commit does not say "this file was renamed".

44
00:03:10,725 --> 00:03:15,894
git diff notices that a path disappeared,
another appeared, and their contents are

45
00:03:15,934 --> 00:03:21,229
similar enough. On screen, one stored file:
run_eval.py, with the ID of its content,

46
00:03:21,269 --> 00:03:21,992
its blob ID.

47
00:03:22,332 --> 00:03:26,708
Precisely. Rename detection is a
pass over the list of changed paths.

48
00:03:26,748 --> 00:03:31,508
A deleted path and an added path with
the same blob ID are an exact rename.

49
00:03:31,548 --> 00:03:36,820
For the remaining pairs Git computes a
similarity index, and a pair at or above the

50
00:03:36,860 --> 00:03:42,003
threshold becomes a rename. On screen the
two blob IDs differ, so this pair needs

51
00:03:42,043 --> 00:03:43,347
the similarity index.

52
00:03:43,687 --> 00:03:49,120
The threshold is 50 percent by default.
-M<n> changes it, and --no-renames switches

53
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:53,537
the pass off. For the porcelain
commands the pass is on by default.

54
00:03:53,577 --> 00:03:58,812
-C also looks for copies, with the sources
limited to files that the same commit

55
00:03:58,852 --> 00:04:04,350
modified. --find-copies-harder, or -C given
twice, considers every file of the older

56
00:04:04,390 --> 00:04:06,130
tree and costs accordingly.

57
00:04:06,470 --> 00:04:11,742
The limit: similarity is a heuristic over
content. Rename a file and rewrite half of

58
00:04:11,782 --> 00:04:16,611
it in the same commit, and the link is
gone. Everything that relies on rename

59
00:04:16,651 --> 00:04:21,859
detection inherits this limit: git log
--follow, git blame, and the rename handling

60
00:04:21,899 --> 00:04:26,917
of git merge, which you met in the merge
module as a modify/delete conflict that

61
00:04:26,957 --> 00:04:27,929
nobody intended.

62
00:04:28,269 --> 00:04:33,935
Noise. Two kinds of change bury the lines
you are looking for: whitespace, and small

63
00:04:33,975 --> 00:04:39,437
edits inside long lines. -w treats lines
that differ only in whitespace as equal.

64
00:04:39,477 --> 00:04:42,902
--word-diff compares words
and marks them in place.

65
00:04:43,242 --> 00:04:47,947
Algorithms. In one sentence: for two given
files there are many correct diffs, and

66
00:04:47,987 --> 00:04:50,320
the algorithm decides which one you read.

67
00:04:50,660 --> 00:04:55,372
Git has four. myers, the default, which
the manual calls "the basic greedy diff

68
00:04:55,412 --> 00:04:59,823
algorithm". minimal, which spends extra
time to produce the smallest diff.

69
00:04:59,863 --> 00:05:04,213
patience. And histogram, which "extends
the patience algorithm to support

70
00:05:04,253 --> 00:05:09,025
low-occurrence common elements". The last
two anchor on lines that occur rarely,

71
00:05:09,065 --> 00:05:13,957
which tends to keep moved blocks together.
Choose with --diff-algorithm=<name> and

72
00:05:13,997 --> 00:05:16,724
make the choice permanent
with diff.algorithm.

73
00:05:17,064 --> 00:05:22,121
--check is not about reading diffs at all.
It inspects the lines a diff adds and

74
00:05:22,161 --> 00:05:27,090
reports whitespace errors and leftover
conflict markers, with an exit status a

75
00:05:27,130 --> 00:05:28,109
script can test.

76
00:05:29,949 --> 00:05:32,289
Sort what a diff tells you into two piles.

77
00:05:32,629 --> 00:05:36,524
Facts: this path exists in the
left tree and not in the right.

78
00:05:36,564 --> 00:05:41,475
These two blobs have different IDs. These
are read from objects, and no option

79
00:05:41,515 --> 00:05:42,301
changes them.

80
00:05:42,641 --> 00:05:47,394
Inferences: "this file was renamed". "These
six lines were edited" as opposed to

81
00:05:47,434 --> 00:05:52,308
"this block moved". "42 lines changed".
Each of those depends on a threshold or an

82
00:05:52,348 --> 00:05:56,801
algorithm, and a different setting gives
a different, equally valid answer.

83
00:05:57,141 --> 00:06:01,079
Think of a map drawn from two aerial
photographs taken a year apart.

84
00:06:01,119 --> 00:06:05,993
The photographs are facts. "This building
was moved" is the cartographer's inference

85
00:06:06,033 --> 00:06:09,619
from "a building vanished here,
a similar one appeared there".

86
00:06:09,659 --> 00:06:13,187
Usually right. Wrong when the
building was also half rebuilt.

87
00:06:13,527 --> 00:06:18,237
Where does the picture break?
A cartographer can go and ask.

88
00:06:18,277 --> 00:06:24,571
Git can't: there's nothing recorded to
ask. So when an inference matters to your

89
00:06:24,611 --> 00:06:30,033
investigation, change the setting
and see whether the answer changes.

90
00:06:31,873 --> 00:06:37,330
One moved and edited file, three ways.
Without rename detection: a deletion and an

91
00:06:37,370 --> 00:06:40,883
addition. By default: one
rename, 82 percent similar.

92
00:06:40,923 --> 00:06:44,302
With word diff: the changed
words, marked in place.

93
00:06:44,342 --> 00:06:46,045
Same two trees every time.

94
00:06:47,885 --> 00:06:49,792
Part 1: from the outside in.

95
00:06:50,132 --> 00:06:55,441
Ten files, 104 insertions, 31 deletions.
One line per file, with a histogram.

96
00:06:55,481 --> 00:07:00,582
You now know where the bulk is before
you have read a single changed line.

97
00:07:03,922 --> 00:07:11,645
--name-status: A for added, M for modified,
and one D: scorekit/bleu.py was deleted

98
00:07:11,685 --> 00:07:13,890
between v0.1.0 and main.

99
00:07:14,230 --> 00:07:18,349
--numstat gives the form for
scripts: added, removed, path.

100
00:07:18,689 --> 00:07:24,831
--diff-filter=A lists only additions, =D
only deletions, and lowercase ad excludes

101
00:07:24,871 --> 00:07:31,089
both and leaves the modifications.
--diff-filter=D with git log is the standard way

102
00:07:31,129 --> 00:07:35,235
to find when a file was
deleted. A later video uses it.

103
00:07:35,575 --> 00:07:40,217
A pathspec, a pattern that limits a command
to some paths, after the double dash

104
00:07:40,257 --> 00:07:43,201
narrows any of these,
including the exclusion form.

105
00:07:43,541 --> 00:07:48,321
Part 2: renames are computed.
labs/run ch14a/diff-renames.

106
00:07:48,361 --> 00:07:55,134
Between commit d369eac and v0.1.0 the
sources moved into a package, one module was

107
00:07:55,174 --> 00:08:00,120
renamed a second time, and two
files were edited on the way.

108
00:08:00,460 --> 00:08:06,411
R100 is an exact rename: two commits moved
scorer.py twice and never changed it, and

109
00:08:06,451 --> 00:08:12,117
the diff of the endpoints sees one rename.
R090 and R082 are renames with edits.

110
00:08:12,157 --> 00:08:16,611
Predict the same comparison with
--no-renames. Say it out loud.

111
00:08:16,651 --> 00:08:17,324
I'll wait.

112
00:08:22,164 --> 00:08:27,310
A list of unrelated deletions and additions.
Same two trees, same facts, different

113
00:08:27,350 --> 00:08:29,397
inference. The threshold decides.

114
00:08:29,737 --> 00:08:34,715
At 90 percent the runner, whose similarity
is 82 percent, is no longer a rename:

115
00:08:34,755 --> 00:08:35,844
it's a D and an A.

116
00:08:36,184 --> 00:08:41,638
In a patch, a detected rename is printed as
extended header lines, "similarity index

117
00:08:41,678 --> 00:08:46,086
82%", "rename from", "rename to",
and only the changed lines follow.

118
00:08:46,426 --> 00:08:50,782
And --stat shows a rename in one
line, with the old and the new name.

119
00:08:51,122 --> 00:08:55,928
Copies need the harder search when the
source file was not modified by the same

120
00:08:55,968 --> 00:08:59,914
commit. Here Asha seeded the nightly
data set from the smoke set.

121
00:09:00,254 --> 00:09:05,599
-C alone finds nothing, because
data/smoke.jsonl itself did not change in that

122
00:09:05,639 --> 00:09:11,053
commit. --find-copies-harder finds the
copy with a similarity of 71 percent and

123
00:09:11,093 --> 00:09:14,781
reduces fourteen added lines
to the four that are new.

124
00:09:15,121 --> 00:09:19,249
Now the limit. A file renamed
and half rewritten in one step.

125
00:09:19,289 --> 00:09:22,050
Predict what git status shows. I'll wait.

126
00:09:26,890 --> 00:09:32,388
A deletion and an addition, because status
uses the default threshold and this pair

127
00:09:32,428 --> 00:09:37,926
is 43 percent similar. A lower threshold
still finds it, but nobody reading the log

128
00:09:37,966 --> 00:09:43,197
later will think to pass -M40%. Moving
and tidying in one commit feels natural.

129
00:09:43,237 --> 00:09:45,132
Almost everyone does it once.

130
00:09:45,472 --> 00:09:50,704
Part 3: reading through noise.
labs/run ch14a/diff-words-whitespace.

131
00:09:50,744 --> 00:09:57,216
Commit 9c8df98 ran a formatter over the
sources. That's the commit from the opening.

132
00:09:57,556 --> 00:10:03,949
Without -w: five files, 40 lines.
With -w: three files, 11 lines.

133
00:10:04,289 --> 00:10:10,758
The 40 lines from the opening have shrunk.
Two files drop out entirely: their only

134
00:10:10,798 --> 00:10:15,996
change was the indentation. What
are the eleven lines that remain?

135
00:10:19,336 --> 00:10:23,362
The formatter also replaced
single quotes with double quotes.

136
00:10:23,402 --> 00:10:26,361
That is not whitespace,
so -w cannot hide it.

137
00:10:26,701 --> 00:10:31,379
Remember this split: it returns in the blame
videos, where git blame -w sees through

138
00:10:31,419 --> 00:10:34,750
the indentation and needs a
different option for the quotes.

139
00:10:35,090 --> 00:10:39,833
For prose, configuration strings and long
lines, a line-based diff says "this line

140
00:10:39,873 --> 00:10:42,633
changed" and leaves the
comparison to your eyes.

141
00:10:42,973 --> 00:10:46,292
Two long lines removed, two
long lines added. Try it now.

142
00:10:46,332 --> 00:10:50,239
Thirty seconds: find the difference
in the last pair, without help.

143
00:10:52,579 --> 00:10:57,531
--word-diff marks removed text in square
brackets with minus signs and added text in

144
00:10:57,571 --> 00:11:00,681
braces with plus signs.
"token" became "token-level".

145
00:11:00,721 --> 00:11:04,900
And in the last line, three words
were removed: "lowercases the text,".

146
00:11:04,940 --> 00:11:07,990
In the line-based diff that
removal was hard to see.

147
00:11:08,030 --> 00:11:09,000
Here it's marked.

148
00:11:09,340 --> 00:11:13,252
The output of --word-diff is
for reading, not for git apply.

149
00:11:13,592 --> 00:11:17,597
Part 4: algorithms. labs/run
ch14a/diff-algorithms.

150
00:11:17,637 --> 00:11:22,434
One file, two similar functions,
and an edit that swaps them.

151
00:11:22,474 --> 00:11:24,100
Nothing else changed.

152
00:11:27,440 --> 00:11:31,058
Myers found the shortest edit:
six lines out, six lines in.

153
00:11:31,098 --> 00:11:36,143
It is correct, and it tells a false story,
that someone edited both functions line

154
00:11:36,183 --> 00:11:36,639
by line.

155
00:11:39,979 --> 00:11:44,322
Histogram tells what happened: one function
removed from the top and added at the

156
00:11:44,362 --> 00:11:45,296
bottom, unchanged.

157
00:11:45,636 --> 00:11:51,164
And the line counts differ: 6 and 6
under Myers, 7 and 7 under histogram and

158
00:11:51,204 --> 00:11:52,996
patience. Same two files.

159
00:11:53,336 --> 00:11:55,769
diff.algorithm makes the choice permanent.

160
00:11:56,109 --> 00:12:03,762
Part 5: --check. labs/run ch14a/diff-check.
A line with two trailing spaces in

161
00:12:03,802 --> 00:12:09,482
config.py, a forgotten conflict
marker in docs/metrics.md.

162
00:12:09,822 --> 00:12:13,501
Both reported, with file and
line, and exit status 2.

163
00:12:13,541 --> 00:12:18,062
Now you run git add -A, which
stages everything, and check again.

164
00:12:18,102 --> 00:12:22,132
Quick quiz, two options. Option
one: the same two reports.

165
00:12:22,172 --> 00:12:25,219
Option two: nothing at
all. Say it out loud.

166
00:12:30,059 --> 00:12:35,906
Option two. After git add -A the unstaged
diff is empty, so the first command finds

167
00:12:35,946 --> 00:12:37,537
nothing: exit status 0.

168
00:12:37,877 --> 00:12:42,351
git diff --cached --check inspects
what the next commit would record.

169
00:12:42,391 --> 00:12:47,847
As with every form of git diff, which changes
are inspected depends on the operands.

170
00:12:48,187 --> 00:12:51,979
With two commits as operands it's the form
for continuous integration, the automated

171
00:12:52,019 --> 00:12:52,983
checks on each change.

172
00:12:54,823 --> 00:12:56,377
Six mistakes to watch for.

173
00:12:56,717 --> 00:13:07,354
Reading a large diff top to bottom. Root
cause: a patch is ordered by path, not by

174
00:13:07,394 --> 00:13:17,379
importance; --stat, --name-status and
--diff-filter show where to look first.

175
00:13:17,719 --> 00:13:23,321
Treating "renamed" as a recorded fact. Root
cause: Git stores no renames; the letter

176
00:13:23,361 --> 00:13:28,291
R is an inference from similarity at
or above a threshold, 50% by default.

177
00:13:28,631 --> 00:13:33,310
Moving a file and rewriting it in the
same commit. Root cause: similarity falls

178
00:13:33,350 --> 00:13:38,029
below the threshold, and every tool that
relies on rename detection then sees a

179
00:13:38,069 --> 00:13:39,522
deletion and an addition.

180
00:13:39,862 --> 00:13:45,272
Assuming -w hides everything a formatter
did. Root cause: -w ignores whitespace

181
00:13:45,312 --> 00:13:48,929
only; quote changes and line
re-wrapping are content.

182
00:13:49,269 --> 00:13:54,061
Comparing "lines changed" from two tools.
Root cause: the count is a property of the

183
00:13:54,101 --> 00:13:56,017
diff algorithm, not of the commit.

184
00:13:56,357 --> 00:14:01,133
Running git diff --check after git
add -A and seeing a clean result.

185
00:14:01,173 --> 00:14:06,869
Root cause: the unstaged diff is empty;
--cached inspects what will be committed.

186
00:14:08,709 --> 00:14:13,218
Now, out of the lab. Three rules that a
model-evaluation team can take from this

187
00:14:13,258 --> 00:14:14,412
part of the textbook.

188
00:14:14,752 --> 00:14:19,673
Rename in one commit, edit in the next. A
commit that only moves files shows R100

189
00:14:19,713 --> 00:14:23,899
everywhere, reviews in seconds, and
keeps every history tool working.

190
00:14:23,939 --> 00:14:29,043
The textbook adds two limits that matter on
large changes: the inexact comparison is

191
00:14:29,083 --> 00:14:33,882
quadratic in the number of unpaired files
and is skipped above diff.renameLimit

192
00:14:33,922 --> 00:14:38,965
candidates, 1000 by default according to
the local manual, so a directory move with

193
00:14:39,005 --> 00:14:42,885
hundreds of edited files can
degrade to deletions and additions.

194
00:14:43,225 --> 00:14:48,677
Set diff.algorithm=histogram. Many teams
do, and the ort merge strategy already uses

195
00:14:48,717 --> 00:14:53,451
histogram internally. Remember the
three consequences the textbook lists.

196
00:14:53,491 --> 00:14:58,682
"Lines changed" is a property of the
algorithm. A patch is still valid under any

197
00:14:58,722 --> 00:15:04,043
algorithm, because all of them transform
the same old file into the same new file.

198
00:15:04,083 --> 00:15:09,340
And the algorithm changes what git blame
and git log -L attribute, since both run

199
00:15:09,380 --> 00:15:10,451
diffs internally.

200
00:15:10,791 --> 00:15:15,743
And git diff --cached --check in a pre-commit
hook, a script Git runs before each

201
00:15:15,783 --> 00:15:20,735
commit, with the two-commit form in CI.
It's the same check that the merge module

202
00:15:20,775 --> 00:15:25,789
recommended before concluding a merge:
leftover conflict markers are caught before

203
00:15:25,829 --> 00:15:27,946
they become a syntax error on main.

204
00:15:29,786 --> 00:15:36,117
Your turn. Do Exercise 11.1, Level
1, "Four questions for the log", in

205
00:15:36,157 --> 00:15:40,121
exercises/m11-m15-investigation-recovery.md.

206
00:15:40,461 --> 00:15:41,991
Predict before each command:

207
00:15:42,331 --> 00:15:47,611
Which summary option answers the question
most directly: files, statuses, or counts?

208
00:15:47,951 --> 00:15:52,145
For any path you expect to see
as renamed: exact or with edits?

209
00:15:52,185 --> 00:15:55,774
What would the same comparison
show with --no-renames?

210
00:15:56,114 --> 00:16:00,950
For a reformatting commit: how many files
do you expect to drop out of the stat

211
00:16:00,990 --> 00:16:03,789
under -w, and what kind
of change will remain?

212
00:16:04,129 --> 00:16:09,857
The challenge is Exercise 11.7, Level 3,
""I only moved it"", in the same file.

213
00:16:11,697 --> 00:16:17,062
Q399: "Git stores no renames. Name three
commands whose output depends on rename

214
00:16:17,102 --> 00:16:20,439
detection, and describe a
commit that defeats it."

215
00:16:22,779 --> 00:16:27,503
Answer out loud first. A strong answer
starts by saying what Git does store and how

216
00:16:27,543 --> 00:16:31,979
a rename is inferred from it, with the
threshold. It names three commands from

217
00:16:32,019 --> 00:16:36,571
different areas, so that the examiner sees
you know the heuristic is shared: one

218
00:16:36,611 --> 00:16:40,072
that compares, one that follows
history, one that integrates.

219
00:16:40,112 --> 00:16:44,778
The commit that defeats it should be
described precisely enough that someone could

220
00:16:44,818 --> 00:16:49,541
make it, with a number for the similarity.
Finish with the practice that avoids the

221
00:16:49,581 --> 00:16:50,001
problem.

222
00:16:51,841 --> 00:16:55,968
Let's land this. You should now be
able to say, in your own words:

223
00:16:56,308 --> 00:17:01,884
I read a large diff from the outside
in: --stat and --name-status first,

224
00:17:01,924 --> 00:17:06,174
--diff-filter and a pathspec
to narrow, the patch last.

225
00:17:06,514 --> 00:17:11,339
Git stores no renames. R and C are
computed from similarity with a 50 percent

226
00:17:11,379 --> 00:17:16,583
default threshold, so a move plus a rewrite
in one commit appears as a deletion and

227
00:17:16,623 --> 00:17:17,341
an addition.

228
00:17:17,681 --> 00:17:22,979
-w removes whitespace-only changes, and
--word-diff shows what changed inside a

229
00:17:23,019 --> 00:17:28,317
line. The same change can be displayed
differently by myers and histogram, with

230
00:17:28,357 --> 00:17:33,452
different line counts, and both are
correct. git diff --cached --check finds

231
00:17:33,492 --> 00:17:38,654
whitespace errors and leftover conflict
markers in what I am about to commit.

232
00:17:40,494 --> 00:17:45,044
Read sections 14A.3 to
14A.6 of Chapter 14A.

233
00:17:45,384 --> 00:17:51,359
Today you learned to read a diff from the
outside in, and to tell its facts from its

234
00:17:51,399 --> 00:17:56,802
inferences. Try --stat and -w on a change
of your own before the next video.

235
00:17:56,842 --> 00:18:02,817
